            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing recommendation probabilities**\n\nI’m looking at the probability of a recommended demand state being correct, and it's clear that this isn't a guarantee. The explicit warning suggests that I shouldn't confuse confidence with certainty. I need to forecast using all available information, potentially considering analysts’ recommendations as probabilities. Combining data via a Bayesian approach might help, but I should be aware of correlation issues. I'll explore how to calculate accurate demand predictions based on peer recommendations and confidence levels.**Assessing posterior probabilities**\n\nI'm analyzing how to handle multiplying multiple posterior probabilities when evaluating demand evidence. Since disjoint surveys suggest conditional independence, I can multiply the odds based on priors. If I treat the reports as separate, odds can be calculated using factors to assess confidence. I also need to ensure I'm not double counting evidence from reports while calculating our own likelihood ratios. It feels a bit complex, but I think this method should give a more accurate picture of demand predictions.",
