            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing survey independence**\n\nI’m considering whether the reports are truly independent since they reference the same demand but come from different surveys. If they're based on distinct observations, then they can be viewed as independent. Without an explicit behavioral model, using these reports as forecasts and aggregating them might require caution. Each of the 18 surveys should be counted just once, and if recommendations map to three separate surveys, I need to be careful not to overcount.**Evaluating report observations**\n\nI realize that each report is based on three unique observations, so the posterior odds should reflect those three. Essentially, I want to count each underlying observation only once. It’s important to establish predictive confidence values and convert them to likelihood ratios. The posterior odds divided by the prior gives us this ratio. I’ll compute the precise probabilities using the given decimals and calculate the odds using the formulas, focusing on getting everything right!",
